@page "/docs/typeset"
<PageTitle>Typeset - Blaizio</PageTitle>

@* The typeset guide - and its own best demo: this page's prose is styled by the stylesheet it
   documents (the `typeset typeset-docs` wrapper below, like every guide page). *@
<div class="typeset typeset-docs space-y-10">
    <header class="docs-page-header space-y-2.5">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
            <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Typeset</h1>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Secondary">one CSS file</BzBadge>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Outline">zero specificity</BzBadge>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Outline">streaming-safe</BzBadge>
        </div>
        <p class="max-w-3xl">Typeset styles the HTML you don't write by hand, from a rendered README to a model streaming markdown into a chat bubble. It is one stylesheet driven by three custom properties and wired to your design tokens, so the prose follows your theme without extra configuration.</p>
    </header>

    <section>
        <p>Most text a Blazor app renders never passes through a component. Markdig hands you an HTML string, you drop it in with <code>MarkupString</code>, and out come bare headings, paragraphs, lists and tables with no classes and no design review. The usual fix is a pile of element styles written for the blog, rewritten for the docs and rewritten again for the chat transcript, with each copy drifting from the last.</p>
        <p>The typeset replaces all of those copies with one file. Content inside a <code>typeset</code> wrapper gets the site's typography automatically, and a small preset class tunes it per context. Because the file is installed as source rather than a package, changing how your prose reads means editing CSS you already own.</p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@HeaderPreset" Numbered="false" Language="css" />
        <p>This page is styled the same way: everything you are reading here is classless markup under one wrapper, and so is every other guide on this site. <a href="typeset">Open the designer</a> to build your own.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Rhythm: three knobs, everything derived</h2>
        <p>Typography systems usually fail by offering too much: a size per heading level, a margin per element pairing, a line-height per breakpoint. Every one of those numbers is a decision, and every decision is a place for two pages to disagree. The typeset works from the opposite end. You set three values, and every other size and gap is derived from them:</p>
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
            <BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50 text-start">
                    <BzTableHead>Knob</BzTableHead>
                    <BzTableHead>Default</BzTableHead>
                    <BzTableHead>What it drives</BzTableHead>
                </BzTableRow>
            </BzTableHeader>
            <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell><code>--typeset-size</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>1em</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Body text. Headings, captions, code and table text are em-multiples of it.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell><code>--typeset-leading</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>1.75</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Line-height of running prose.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell><code>--typeset-flow</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>1.25em</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>The base gap between blocks. A heading takes about twice the gap above and half of it below, list items take a fraction, and a rule takes double.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
            </BzTableBody>
        </BzTable>
        <p>Change the flow and the heading spacing scales with it. Shrink the size and the whole hierarchy shrinks in step, because nothing inside is absolute.</p>
        <p>Fonts work the same way. <code>--typeset-font-heading</code> and <code>--typeset-font-mono</code> read your existing <code>--font-heading</code> and <code>--font-mono</code> tokens, body text inherits the page, and every color comes from the theme. That is why the typeset works with all eight styles, every theme and dark mode without a line of configuration.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Installation</h2>
        <p><CmdChip Command="blaizio add typeset" /> writes <code>Styles/typeset.css</code> next to your tokens file and records the install in <code>blaizio.json</code>, so <CmdChip Command="blaizio remove typeset" /> can undo exactly that later. Then import the file in <code>Styles/app.css</code>, after Tailwind:</p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@InstallCss" Numbered="false" Language="css" />
        <p>A plain import is enough because the file wraps itself in <code>@("@layer components")</code>, and Tailwind's utilities live in a later layer that overrides it. Keep the import after Tailwind, though, because importing it first would order its layer below preflight.</p>
        <p>The layer also explains two behaviors. Any utility on an element beats the typeset, and links are styled only when they have no class. A classed anchor, such as a button rendered as an anchor or other interface chrome placed in the prose, is yours to style.</p>
        <p>Finally, wrap whatever renders your markdown. <code>typeset</code> switches the styles on and the preset class carries your values:</p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@WrapSnippet" Numbered="false" Language="html" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Building your typeset</h2>
        <p>The <a href="typeset">designer</a> is the fastest way to build a preset. Pick the measure, the three faces and the rhythm values, and see them applied to three sample documents: an article, a technical guide and a chat answer. Lock the values you have settled and shuffle the rest, with undo and redo for experiments.</p>
        <p>Get code then gives you every piece a project needs: the stylesheet, the webfont link plus <code>--font-*</code> variables for a Blazor host, the preset class and the wrapper. You can take them as numbered steps, or as a single brief to paste into a coding agent.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Defaults and presets</h2>
        <p>Bare <code>typeset</code> works with no preset at all, using the values the file ships with:</p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@DefaultsCss" Numbered="false" Language="css" />
        <p>Two defaults matter more than they look:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><b>Size is <code>1em</code>, not a pixel value.</b> The typeset inherits the type scale of wherever it lands, smaller inside a chat bubble and larger in an article column. On viewports under 40rem it increases slightly for reading at arm's length.</li>
            <li><b>No max-width.</b> Line length belongs to your layout, so the stylesheet never sets one. The designer's Measure knob puts a <code>max-w-[70ch]</code> on the wrapper markup instead, where you can see it and change it.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Presets let one app serve several reading contexts. This site's docs use one, and a chat surface could sit beside it with tighter spacing:</p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@PresetCss" Numbered="false" Language="css" />
        <CodeBlock Code="@PresetUse" Numbered="false" Language="html" />
        <p>For a one-off, skip the preset entirely. The three values are plain custom properties, so Tailwind's bracket notation can set them inline:</p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@InlineOverride" Numbered="false" Language="html" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Presets for different kinds of reading</h2>
        <p>Because the font faces are custom properties too, a preset can change the whole character of the content, not just its density. A serif preset for long reads and a dense reference preset can share one stylesheet:</p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@ThemesCss" Numbered="false" Language="css" />
        <p>The same approach helps with accessibility: publish a roomier preset and let readers pick it in a settings menu.</p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@LargeCss" Numbered="false" Language="css" />
        <p>Dark mode needs nothing extra, because the tokens switch and the typeset follows. If prose feels cramped on the dark surface, increase the leading only there:</p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@DarkCss" Numbered="false" Language="css" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Wide tables</h2>
        <p>Markdown tables render as real tables and wrap their cells to fit the column. When wrapping would make a table unreadable, as with an API matrix or a long option list, put it inside a <code>typeset-scroll</code> element. The table then scrolls sideways inside its own box while the page stays put:</p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@ScrollSnippet" Numbered="false" Language="html" />
        <p>Emit the wrapper from wherever your pipeline builds tables (a Markdig extension, your renderer's table template). The wrapper contains any over-wide block the same way, not just tables.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Escaping the typeset</h2>
        <p>There are two ways out, for two different situations:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><b>Override one element.</b> Every typeset rule is wrapped in <code>:where()</code>, which reduces its specificity to zero. Any utility on any element wins without <code>!important</code>, and plain CSS with an ordinary selector wins the same way. Zero specificity is also why Blaizio components placed in the prose keep their look: their own classes outrank rules with no weight.</li>
            <li><b>Exempt a subtree.</b> Mark an element <code>not-typeset</code> (or <code>data-not-typeset</code>) and the typeset skips it and everything inside. The nearest wrapper wins, so starting a fresh <code>typeset</code> inside an opted-out subtree resumes styling there. An opted-out card can still hold a typeset chat transcript. This site does both: demo shells and code surfaces opt out, and the chat demos on the Message pages opt back in inside them.</li>
        </ul>
        <CodeBlock Code="@EscapeSnippet" Numbered="false" Language="html" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Streaming</h2>
        <p>A chat transcript appends blocks while the reader watches. Under most prose stylesheets each new block restyles the one before it: a last-child margin collapses or a separator jumps, and the message visibly twitches as it grows. The typeset's layout rules are written so that cannot happen:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><b>No rule depends on what comes next.</b> <code>:last-child</code>, <code>:has()</code> and <code>:empty</code> are never used in layout rules, because what they match changes when content arrives.</li>
            <li><b>Spacing only looks backward.</b> Every gap is a <code>margin-block-start</code> that applies only when a previous sibling exists, so a new block brings its own spacing and changes nothing above it.</li>
            <li><b>Table separators belong to existing rows.</b> An appended row never redraws its neighbor.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Streaming text still grows and wraps normally. The guarantee is only that finished blocks stay finished. Pair it with <a href="docs/components/message">Message</a> and <a href="docs/components/message-scroller">Message Scroller</a> for the rest of a chat surface. Both pages carry a live typeset demo. On Message, the prose sits in a ghost bubble. On Message Scroller, blocks stream into a wrapper, and you can watch that nothing above them moves.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Relationship to @("@tailwindcss/typography")</h2>
        <p>If you have used the <a href="https://github.com/tailwindlabs/tailwindcss-typography" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>prose</code> plugin</a>, the typeset will feel familiar, because it deliberately keeps the plugin's two best ideas: the zero-specificity guard and the opt-out class. It departs wherever the two tools' goals differ. The plugin ships a fixed scale as a generated npm artifact, while the typeset is a container-relative system that reads your tokens, is tuned per context, stays stable while streaming and is delivered as source you edit.</p>
        <div class="typeset-scroll">
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th></th>
                        <th>@("@tailwindcss/typography")</th>
                        <th>Typeset</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Scale</td><td>Fixed rem steps (<code>prose-sm</code> ... <code>prose-2xl</code>)</td><td>Em-relative to wherever it sits</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Dark mode</td><td>Second palette via <code>prose-invert</code></td><td>Automatic: the tokens switch underneath it</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Theming</td><td>Its own color variables, scale baked in</td><td>Your tokens, plus face and rhythm knobs</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Tweaking</td><td>Modifier API (<code>prose-a:</code>, <code>prose-headings:</code>)</td><td>Any utility or plain selector outranks it</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Streaming</td><td>Not a design goal</td><td>Append-stable by contract</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Ships as</td><td>npm plugin generating CSS</td><td>A file in your repo, via <code>blaizio add</code></td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </section>
</div>

@code {
    private const string HeaderPreset =
        """
        .typeset-docs {
          --typeset-size: 15px;
          --typeset-leading: 1.65;
          --typeset-flow: 1.25em;
        }
        """;

    private const string InstallCss =
        """
        /* Styles/app.css */
        @import "tailwindcss";
        @import "./typeset.css";
        """;

    private const string WrapSnippet =
        """
        <article class="typeset typeset-docs max-w-[70ch]">
            @((MarkupString)Markdown.ToHtml(content))
        </article>
        """;

    private const string DefaultsCss =
        """
        .typeset {
          --typeset-font-body: inherit;
          --typeset-font-heading: var(--font-heading);
          --typeset-font-mono: var(--font-mono);

          --typeset-size: 1em;      /* body font-size */
          --typeset-leading: 1.75;  /* line-height */
          --typeset-flow: 1.25em;   /* space between blocks */
        }
        """;

    private const string PresetCss =
        """
        .typeset-docs {
          --typeset-size: 15px;
          --typeset-flow: 1.5em;
        }

        .typeset-chat {
          --typeset-flow: 1em;
          --typeset-leading: 1.6;
        }
        """;

    private const string PresetUse =
        """
        <article class="typeset typeset-docs">@pageHtml</article>
        <div class="typeset typeset-chat">@messageHtml</div>
        """;

    private const string InlineOverride =
        """
        <article class="typeset [--typeset-flow:1.75em] [--typeset-size:17px]">...</article>
        """;

    private const string ThemesCss =
        """
        /* Long reads: serif faces, generous rhythm. */
        .typeset-longread {
          --typeset-font-body: var(--font-lora);
          --typeset-font-heading: var(--font-lora);
          --typeset-size: 18px;
          --typeset-leading: 1.9;
          --typeset-flow: 2em;
        }

        /* Reference: compact, information-dense. */
        .typeset-reference {
          --typeset-size: 13px;
          --typeset-leading: 1.55;
          --typeset-flow: 0.875em;
        }
        """;

    private const string LargeCss =
        """
        .typeset-comfort {
          --typeset-size: 17px;
          --typeset-leading: 2;
          --typeset-flow: 1.75em;
        }
        """;

    private const string DarkCss =
        """
        .dark .typeset {
          --typeset-leading: 1.9;
        }
        """;

    private const string ScrollSnippet =
        """
        <div class="typeset-scroll">
            <table>...</table>
        </div>
        """;

    private const string EscapeSnippet =
        """
        <div class="typeset typeset-docs">
            <p>Typeset prose.</p>
            <p class="text-lg text-primary">One element, overridden by utilities.</p>
            <BzCard Class="not-typeset">A whole subtree, exempt.</BzCard>
        </div>
        """;
}
